WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.176
Some adversaries deliberately study industrial environments and design activity around process operations.

00:00:05.176 --> 00:00:10.252
Their objective may be espionage, strategic access, loss of control, reduced reliability,

00:00:10.252 --> 00:00:11.521
or physical disruption.

00:00:11.521 --> 00:00:15.751
These threats are less common than routine malware and misconfiguration,

00:00:15.751 --> 00:00:19.981
but their potential consequence justifies focused preparation in high-impact environments.

00:00:19.981 --> 00:00:25.057
Process knowledge can increase an adversary's impact because the meaning of a

00:00:25.057 --> 00:00:27.171
command depends on equipment state,

00:00:27.171 --> 00:00:29.286
timing, safeguards, and operator response.

00:00:29.286 --> 00:00:32.670
Safety-related systems deserve particular protection and independent analysis.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:37.746
Defenders should avoid assuming that separation by name alone makes a safety

00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:40.707
function unreachable or immune from shared dependencies.

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:47.052
Advanced actors may also use legitimate administration and engineering capabilities already present in the environment.

00:00:47.052 --> 00:00:50.859
This is often described as living off the land.

00:00:50.859 --> 00:00:56.358
The defensive implication is that detecting a known malware file is not enough.

00:00:56.358 --> 00:01:01.011
Teams need visibility into who used sensitive tools, from which workstation,

00:01:01.011 --> 00:01:03.972
against which asset, under what change record,

00:01:03.972 --> 00:01:06.933
and whether the activity matched expected operations.

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:10.740
High-level threat modeling can identify critical functions, engineering pathways,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:11.586
trust boundaries,

00:01:11.586 --> 00:01:17.508
and conditions that could lead to unsafe or unreliable operation without documenting harmful procedures.

00:01:17.508 --> 00:01:20.046
Monitoring should prioritize unexpected configuration activity,

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:23.006
unusual cross-level communications, changes to trusted baselines,

00:01:23.006 --> 00:01:25.121
and access outside approved windows.

00:01:25.121 --> 00:01:27.659
Resilience requires independent safeguards, segmented architecture,

00:01:27.659 --> 00:01:30.620
protected engineering systems, recoverable configurations, coordinated response,

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:35.273
and operators trained to recognize loss of trusted control or visibility.

00:01:35.273 --> 00:01:39.503
Advanced-threat preparation should strengthen defenses against ordinary incidents as well.

00:01:39.503 --> 00:01:41.618
Clear ownership, strong access control,

00:01:41.618 --> 00:01:47.540
and evidence-based change management reduce the opportunity for both sophisticated actors and routine mistakes.
